3. IAM Policy

A Policy is a JSON document defining permissions.

Example:

{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect":"Allow",
      "Action":"s3:GetObject",
      "Resource":"arn:aws:s3:::mybucket/*"
    }
  ]
}

This policy allows reading objects from a specific S3 bucket.

Policy Structure

Every policy has:

Version

Policy language version.

"Version":"2012-10-17"
Effect

Can be:

Allow

or

Deny
Action

Which AWS API operations are permitted?

Examples:

ec2:StartInstances

ec2:StopInstances

s3:GetObject

s3:PutObject
Resource

Specifies which resources are affected.

Example:

arn:aws:s3:::company-backup/*
Condition (Optional)

Apply permissions only when specific conditions are met.

Example:

Only allow access from a corporate IP address.

Types of IAM Policies
AWS Managed Policies

Created and maintained by AWS.

Examples:

AmazonS3ReadOnlyAccess
AdministratorAccess
AmazonEC2FullAccess

Advantages:

Easy to use
Automatically updated
Customer Managed Policies

Created by your organization.

Advantages:

More secure
Tailored to business needs
Easier to reuse
Inline Policies

Attached directly to one user, group, or role.

Characteristics:

Cannot be shared
Deleted with the identity

Usually used only for special cases.

IAM Roles

A Role provides temporary credentials.

Unlike a User:

